{"name":"White Heat","hex":"#fdf9ef","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white heat","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The state or temperature at which matter emits white light.","A state of intensive activity."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white_heat"}],"entries":[{"title":"White Heat","blocks":[{"text":"White Heat is a 1949 American film noir, directed by Raoul Walsh. Its screenplay, written by Ivan Goff and Ben Roberts, is based on a story by Virginia Kellogg. The film stars James Cagney, Virginia Mayo, and Edmond O'Brien."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/White_Heat_%281949_poster%29.jpg/330px-White_Heat_%281949_poster%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Heat","wikidata":"Q607337"},{"title":"White Heat (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"White Heat is a British television drama series, written by Paula Milne, and first broadcast on BBC Two from 8 March to 12 April 2012. The series follows seven students who first meet in a London, Tufnell Park flatshare in 1965 and consists of six one-hour episodes, set in 1965, 1967, 1973, 1979, 1982 and 1990."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Heat_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q7994783"},{"title":"Heat","blocks":[{"text":"In thermodynamics, heat is defined as energy in transfer between a body and its surroundings, other than through thermodynamic work or through transfer of matter."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Glowing_metal.jpg/330px-Glowing_metal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heat","wikidata":"Q44432"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/James_Cagney_in_White_Heat_trailer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/James_Cagney_in_White_Heat_trailer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"James Cagney in the film's opening robbery scene","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJames_Cagney_in_White_Heat_trailer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Virginia_Mayo_and_James_Cagney_in_White_Heat_trailer.jpg/250px-Virginia_Mayo_and_James_Cagney_in_White_Heat_trailer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1d/Virginia_Mayo_and_James_Cagney_in_White_Heat_trailer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Virginia_Mayo_and_James_Cagney_in_White_Heat_trailer.jpg/250px-Virginia_Mayo_and_James_Cagney_in_White_Heat_trailer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Virginia Mayo and James Cagney","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVirginia_Mayo_and_James_Cagney_in_White_Heat_trailer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/de/Prisonscene.whiteheat.cagney.png/500px-Prisonscene.whiteheat.cagney.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=par